+#### Max Consecutive Losses
+
+`MaxConsecutiveLosses` looks at the most recently closed trades for a pair (within `lookback_period` in minutes, or in candles when using `lookback_period_candles`) and walks backward from the latest trade as long as trades keep losing.
+If `trade_limit` or more losing trades occurred **in a row** (an unbroken streak ending with the most recent trade), the pair is locked for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`). A single winning trade resets the streak.
+
+Unlike `StoplossGuard` and `LowProfitPairs`, which count how many qualifying trades fall within a window, `MaxConsecutiveLosses` specifically requires the losses to be consecutive - so a losing streak interrupted by even one winning trade will not trigger it.
+
+This protection also supports a bot-wide (global) mode: by default it evaluates the consecutive-loss streak across all pairs combined, stopping new entries everywhere once the streak reaches `trade_limit`. Set `only_per_pair` to `true` to restrict it to per-pair streaks only, without ever locking globally.
+
+For futures bots, setting `only_per_side` will make the bot only consider one side, and will then only lock this one side, allowing for example shorts to continue after a series of losing longs.
+
+`required_profit` determines the relative profit (or loss) below which a trade counts as a loss. This should normally not be set and defaults to 0.0 - meaning any trade that closed with a negative profit counts towards the streak.
+
+The below example stops trading a pair for 4 candles once it has lost 3 times in a row within the last 24 candles.
+
+``` python
+@property
+def protections(self):
+    return [
+        {
+            "method": "MaxConsecutiveLosses",
+            "lookback_period_candles": 24,
+            "trade_limit": 3,
+            "stop_duration_candles": 4,
+            "required_profit": 0.0,
+            "only_per_pair": False,
+            "only_per_side": False
+        }
+    ]
+```
+
 ### Full example of Protections
 
 All protections can be combined at will, also with different parameters, creating a increasing wall for under-performing pairs.
